{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791624460,"nonce":"TF8sfk15Z4tSeFBb/M8ZXcD/qjw=","title":"Spring timing for transitions and animations","message":"The motion engine (native/src/motion, part of 35f7bd0c1ec8685e615f6ffdd92e84d69d9de4ea0a5820e8da6694a414ca344f) times everything with CSS easing functions: keywords, cubic-bezier() and steps(). The theme's --ease-spring is one of those, so a spring is approximated by an overshooting cubic-bezier and has no physics: no mass, stiffness or damping, no settling time, and an interrupted spring restarts its curve instead of keeping its velocity.\n\nashui has an RK4 spring (haxe/ashui/animation/{Spring,SpringConfig,AnimationScheduler}.hx) that its popovers and badges use. Here a spring should stay time-sampled like the rest of the engine (value at now, no stepping thread): a closed-form damped oscillator per field, its duration the time it takes to settle, carrying velocity across an interruption.\n\nOpen question: how CSS names it. linear() can approximate a spring curve today and is a CSS standard; a spring() timing function or a theme token that expands to one are the alternatives.\n\nAcceptance: a transition timed by the spring overshoots and settles by the parameters given; reversed mid-flight it continues with its velocity rather than from rest; checked frame by frame in tests/native-motion-window.mjs.","files":null}]}